# Review: `yin.vm.implementation-plan.md` and `yin.repl.implementation-plan.md`

**Verified first, because the plans' credibility rests on it:** the line counts in the closure table are exact (91/258/231/554/282/47/59/671/154/795 = 3,142); `semantic.cljc:640,694,1118` really do call `dao.space.query`/`dao.space.transact` (1,503 + 382 lines); the six closure files really have zero `dao.space` references; `yin.module` requires only `clojure.string`; `dao.datom` has zero stream references; the "fifteen consumers" count is exactly right (fifteen `src/` files outside the vm tree require `yin.vm`); every `repl.cljc`, `rpc/*`, `ws.cljc` line citation in the two plans checks out against the source, including `ws.cljc:68` (the hidden `:remote-stream` inbox read), `ws.cljc:396-408` (cljd `listen!` never sets `:socket-close-fn`), `rpc/server.cljc:65` (discarded `put-response!` result), `repl_test.cljc:320/581`, the `deps.edn`/`shadow-cljs.edn`/`runner.clj:13` build claims, and `engine.cljc:310` (the ingress guard). The plans are unusually honest documents. The findings below are about the two places where the honesty stops: the closure claim is false in one particular, and the "what actually changes" list is missing the port's hardest third.

## Findings, ranked

**1 | Critical | `yin.vm.implementation-plan.md:33-36,38-49` vs `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:7-9`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:7`, `src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:10,387,457-464`** — Claim: "Every v1 dependency is either `dao.stream` itself … or a namespace in this table. Nothing dangles." | Correction: `dao.stream.ringbuffer` dangles. Both `vm.cljc` (all three host branches, lines 7–9) and `engine.cljc` (unconditionally, line 7) require it, and it is neither `dao.stream` nor in the table. The fix is not cosmetic, because the dependency is not passive: (a) `vm.cljc:137` calls `ringbuffer/make-ring-buffer-stream` directly — a v1 constructor with no v2 equivalent; (b) constructing any ring buffer lazily registers the `stream` module — `make`, `put!`, `cursor`, `next!`, `take!`, `close!` — into `yin.module`'s global registry (`ringbuffer.cljc:387,457-464`), and that module is what `yang.clojure:24-25` compiles `stream/make`, `>!`, `<!` calls against. The v2 ring buffer (sibling Phase 2) must not require `yin.module` — that is the v1 cycle this plan refuses to reproduce (plan:225-226; precisely: `dao.stream.ringbuffer` → `yin.module`, not "dao.stream.cljc → yin.vm" as the plan has it). So the table gains a row mapping `dao.stream.ringbuffer` → `dao.stream.ringbuffer` (sibling Phase 2), the constructor call is rewritten to `create!` with a spec, and `yin.vm` is assigned module-registration duty — otherwise `(require '[stream])` silently resolves nothing in every v2 REPL. The scope, line count, and independence claims all survive this; the "nothing dangles" sentence does not.

**2 | Critical | `yin.vm.implementation-plan.md:60-100,149-184` vs `engine.cljc:442-454,484-501,156-159,223-227,237,455-465,531-541`, `runtime.cljc:74-134,156-158,181-183,189,197,219-221,115`, `ffi.cljc:111-114`, `ast_walker.cljc:139-140`** — Claim: six v1 idioms, and "finding them all is most of the work." | Correction: the list omits the v1 readiness/wake protocol wholesale, which is the port's largest redesign problem: `satisfies? ds/IDaoStreamWaitable` with `register-reader-waiter!`/`register-writer-waiter!` (engine:442-454, 484-501; runtime:156-158, 181-183; ast_walker:139-140); `:woke` consumed from `append!`/`close!` results (engine:159, 225, 237, 455-465, 520-541; runtime:189, 219-221; ffi:111-114) — v2's `append!` outcome carries no `:woke` and `close!`'s outcome set is `{ok}` only; `ds/drain-one!` — a destructive read the contract lists under Explicitly Absent — in `handle-take`/`:stream/take` (engine:223) and `runtime:94,197`; and `ds/closed?` (runtime:115). The port must drop transport-local waiters in favor of the scheduler's wait-set fallback, re-derive close-waking (a parked `:stream/next` reader on a closed stream now wakes only when `check-wait-set` retries `next` and reads `end`), and either delete `:stream/take` or redefine it over cursor+`next`. It also undercounts the first idiom: fabricated cursors and `:position` arithmetic live not just at `stream_driver.cljc:25` and `apply.cljc:170,184` but at `vm.cljc:542-545` (empty-state), `engine.cljc:24-31,108-128,162-171,191,253-264`, `ast_walker.cljc:786`, `ffi.cljc:31-32,83,96` — the VM store's whole cursor representation must become opaque stream-minted values, and the `(inc pos)` arithmetic in `make-woken-run-queue-entries` and `rt-result->vm-result` has no mechanical mapping. Add a seventh idiom: telemetry classifies cursors structurally (`cursor-map?`, `telemetry.cljc:131-133,168-172`) and v2 cursors are opaque with no recognition predicate in the contract — the summary degrades to `:opaque` unless the VM tags its own cursor-refs.

**3 | Critical | `yin.vm.implementation-plan.md:80-84,182-184` vs `ringbuffer.cljc:16,60-91`, `engine.cljc:135-141,156-159`, `test/yin/vm/engine_test.cljc:263,365,386,419`, `dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:139-141`, `yin.repl.implementation-plan.md:388-389`** — Claim: Phase V4 delivers "the same results the v1 ast-walker produces for the same programs." | Correction: it cannot, because v1 VM-store streams are **reject-mode** ring buffers (`default-eviction-policy :reject`, `ringbuffer.cljc:16`; `handle-make` passes no policy, `engine.cljc:137`), so `:stream/put` on a full stream parks the continuation — backpressure, tested at `engine_test.cljc:263,365`. The only v2 ring buffer is evict-oldest with `append!` never `full` (sibling plan:139-141; the REPL plan itself admits at 388-389 no other mode exists). Ported as specified, puts never park and slow readers get gaps: a program-visible semantic change the plan never makes. Either extend sibling Phase 2 with a reject-mode stream (with reasons, per the contract's outcome-declaration rule) or write down the divergence and delete the "same results" sentence. The same hole hits the FFI plumbing: v1's call-in/call-out are unbounded (`vm.cljc:139`, `apply.cljc:35-36`); v2 has no unbounded mode, and eviction there means silently lost host calls — no capacity, no gap policy, nothing is stated for them.

**4 | High | `yin.vm.implementation-plan.md:80-84,141-142,157-159`** — Claim: "`vm.cljc:139` … **the VM's in-stream is created with a declared capacity**, recorded in this plan as 4096." | Correction: `vm.cljc:139` is `open-local-stream`, which creates the **dao.stream.apply call-in/call-out plumbing** wired into `empty-state` (`vm.cljc:539-545`) — not the in-stream. The in-stream is caller-supplied (`ast_walker.cljc:782`; created by the REPL's `make-vm`, `repl.cljc:169,344-346`, unbounded in v1). The 4096 decision therefore lands on the wrong stream in the wrong plan, and the streams `vm.cljc:139` actually creates get no decision at all (finding 3). Likewise V2's "yin.vm.telemetry — … its stream is created with a declared capacity" — in v1 the VM never creates the telemetry stream; the REPL does (`repl.cljc:606-621`; `telemetry/normalize-config` demands a supplied `:stream`, `telemetry.cljc:32-38`). Attribute each capacity to its actual creator.

**5 | High | `yin.vm.implementation-plan.md:116-120` + `yin.repl.implementation-plan.md:211-215,359-363,317-319,351`** — Claim: the two envelopes "should agree… under their own keys"; R1 fixes its envelope "agreed with `dao.stream.apply`'s"; "Each keeps its own key namespace"; "R1 and R2 are executable today." | Correction: the agreement is bidirectionally deferred with no arbiter, and the sequencing makes it worse, not better: R1 executes before V3 exists (REPL plan:317-319 — the VM plan "can be built in parallel with this plan's R1"), so R1's first bullet either invents the envelope unilaterally — the exact defect both plans name — or blocks on a later phase of the other plan. "Each keeps its own key namespace" is the duplication restated: two vocabularies for `{id, op, args}` and `{id, value}`. The v1 precedent answers the ownership question: `dao.stream.rpc.client` **requires** `dao.stream.apply` and uses its constructors (`rpc/client.cljc:17`). Make it one envelope in `dao.stream.apply`, have `dao.stream.rpc` require it and add only correlation matching, lifecycle decode, conservative loss, and rebind — and move `dao.stream.rpc.client/server` into the VM plan's V3 (they are socket-free, exactly V3's register) so the REPL plan's R1 shrinks to `rpc.ws`'s `:decode` and tests. That resolves the deadlock and fixes the sequencing lie: as written, "R1 and R2 are executable today" is true for R1 only after this repair, and false for R2 in any case (its prerequisite — the whole VM plan through V4 plus stream Phases 1–2 — exists nowhere under `src/`; no v2 code has been written).

**6 | High | `yin.repl.implementation-plan.md:65-69` vs `repl.cljc:183`, `yang.clojure:20-22,120-122`, `semantic.cljc:558-559,825`, `ast_walker.cljc:326-456`** — Claim: "`(vm :type)` accepts only `:ast-walker` … This is the one user-visible reduction against v1." | Correction: there are three. (a) v1's default VM is `:semantic` (`repl.cljc:183`), which expands `:yin/macro-expand` at runtime (`semantic.cljc:558-559,825`); the ast-walker has no `:yin/macro-expand` case and throws "Unknown AST node type" (`ast_walker.cljc:455-456`), while `yang.clojure` compiles every user-`defmacro` call site to exactly that node (`yang.clojure:20-22`). `defn` survives only via its native compile path (`yang.clojure:120-122`). So user-defined macros stop evaluating, and the default evaluator changes. (b) The `stream` module surface — `(stream/take! s)` above all, which bottoms out in the retired `drain-one!` — has no v2 semantics (findings 1, 2). (c) The evaluator-set reduction itself. The scope decision (ast-walker, not semantic) is right — the `dao.space` reasoning verifies exactly — but "one user-visible reduction" is not.

**7 | Medium | `yin.vm.implementation-plan.md:136-147` vs `runtime.cljc:56-134,141-221`** — Claim: Phase V1's three namespaces have "no v2-specific behaviour of their own"; `dao.runtime` is "the ready-queue and wait-set state, with its single `dao.stream` reference moved to v2." | Correction: that counts the `require`, not the use. Half of `dao.runtime` is v1-stream machinery — `check-wait-set` (`ds/next` with `:position` bookkeeping at 74-93, `ds/closed?` at 115, `ds/drain-one!` at 94), `handle-read`/`handle-write` (waitable registration, 156-158/181-183), `handle-take` (`drain-one!`, 197), `handle-close` (`:woke`, 219-221). `dao.runtime` is a redesign scheduled in the wrong phase; move it to V2 beside the stream driver and re-estimate it.

**8 | Medium | `yin.vm.implementation-plan.md:186-203`** — Claim: fresh tests "mirroring the v1 suite's coverage for the subsystems in scope," and **no parity test** because it "would require both VMs loadable in one process … the coupling that would create is exactly what this plan exists to avoid." | Correction: the refusal's premise misreads the plan's own end condition. The constraint (plan:230-233) binds namespaces under `yin.vm`/`dao.*.v2` — a test namespace is neither, and coexistence already places both trees in one codebase with v1 consumers running beside v2; one process can load both. A parity suite (same corpus through both walkers, compare plain values) creates no production coupling and is the strongest available evidence — and the plan needs it, because it simultaneously asserts "the same results the v1 ast-walker produces" (182-184) with no mechanism that could check it, while finding 3 guarantees at least one real divergence. Keep the fresh suite, add the parity suite, and make the intentional divergences a written register. Relatedly, "mirroring the v1 suite's coverage" is unsatisfiable as stated: `engine_test.cljc:263,365,386,419` assert park-on-`full`, close-wakes-writer, and take-wakes-reader — v1-only semantics. Say which behaviors are deliberately not mirrored; that list is the divergence register.

**9 | Low | `yin.repl.implementation-plan.md:299-312,419-434`** — Claim (D4): "the existing tests … send sequentially (`repl_test.cljc:581`), so they never settled it." | Correction: accurate, but the plan then asserts two clients "each get their own answers" (R5 fact 1, plan:444) without noting that this is a *new* guarantee v1 never had and D4's single serial shell does not obviously provide — with one shared REPL state and one driver, fact 1 needs a definition of "own answer" (reply routed by attachment) that R4's wiring implies but never states. One sentence in R4 would close it.

